WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.720
In 1290, Prince Ludwig of Bavaria died under&nbsp;
suspicious circumstances during a tournament.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.880
A nobleman from Hohenlohe had struck him in the&nbsp;
neck during a joust and mortally wounded him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.160
even though the two had agreed to fight&nbsp;
with blunt weapons. Suspicions immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:27.440
arose that Ludwig’s opponent had cheated or even&nbsp;
intentionally injured him. This incident does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:34.080
fit with our modern image of medieval tournaments.&nbsp;
We think of gleaming armor, courtly splendor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:41.040
and noble knights fighting according to fixed&nbsp;
rules for glory and the favor of a lady. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.600
this image has a historical core, it is heavily&nbsp;
romanticized and embellished. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.520
tournaments were more varied, often more&nbsp;
dangerous, and less strictly regulated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:01.120
They were not merely chivalric contests; there was&nbsp;
a great deal at stake: people fought for status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:08.400
glory, and, not least, money. The temptation&nbsp;
to gain an unfair advantage was therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.160
strong—and there were more than enough ways&nbsp;
to do so: from tampering with armor and using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.080
forbidden weapons to rigging lances—as may&nbsp;
have been the case with Ludwig of Bavaria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.680
but more on that later. In this video, we’ll&nbsp;
take a look at how fair medieval tournaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:34.880
really were and whether there was cheating.
How fair were medieval tournaments really?&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.920
The earliest tournaments were virtually&nbsp;
indistinguishable from a battle. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.720
nothing in common with the romanticized&nbsp;
spectacle we know from medieval films;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.080
rather, they served as training for war&nbsp;
and were therefore designed to recreate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.280
conditions of war as realistically as possible.
The first such tournaments can be traced back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:02.160
to the 11th century in northern France. Up to 200&nbsp;
horsemen on each side competed against one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.320
across a tournament field that sometimes spanned&nbsp;
several square kilometers and included streams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:14.640
forests, and meadows. They fought with the same&nbsp;
weapons and wore the same armor they used in war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.240
and serious injuries and deaths were common.&nbsp;
The fighting generally followed the pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:26.400
of an actual battle: The tournament began with a&nbsp;
coordinated cavalry charge, followed by prolonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:34.000
close-quarters combat. There were no referees,&nbsp;
and usually only two rules: Both sides had a base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.160
a protected zone where they could not be attacked,&nbsp;
and it was frowned upon to intentionally kill an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.800
opponent. This type of tournament spread rapidly&nbsp;
throughout Europe and became an integral part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.760
chivalric culture—even though the Church and&nbsp;
some rulers occasionally attempted to ban it.&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.600
What distinguished this early tournament from a&nbsp;
battle was the motivation behind the fighting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.680
The purpose was not to settle a conflict, but&nbsp;
to fight, train, gain honor, and take plunder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.800
Winning tournaments could make a man rich because&nbsp;
he could take prisoners and demand ransom for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.880
them. William Marshal, who later became the&nbsp;
Earl of Pembroke, was one of Europe’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:24.000
successful tournament knights and is said to have&nbsp;
captured a total of around 500 other knights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.960
This earned him a fortune, because he&nbsp;
could usually keep his opponent’s armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:37.040
horse, and weapons and demand a high ransom&nbsp;
for his release—often an entire year’s income.&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:44.720
Because so much was at stake, everyone naturally&nbsp;
tried to win and gain an advantage. Since there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:52.080
were hardly any rules, there was considerable room&nbsp;
for interpretation as to how far one could go—or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:59.120
more precisely, where smart tactics ended and&nbsp;
unfair behavior began. A good example of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:06.480
Philip of Flanders, who was, in fact, a medieval&nbsp;
“camper.” When he arrived at the tournament field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.840
he let it be known that he had come only as&nbsp;
a spectator and waited with his men at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.960
assembly point until the organized cavalry charge&nbsp;
was over and close combat had begun. Only then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.240
once his opponents were scattered, did Philip&nbsp;
suddenly lead his men into the tournament after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:31.600
all, overrunning his exhausted opponents and&nbsp;
taking scores of prisoners. Although the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.800
participants did not necessarily approve of&nbsp;
this tactic, they apparently accepted it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:43.840
skillful use of the leeway allowed by the rules,&nbsp;
for Philip was regarded as one of Europe’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.320
exemplary knights (fleur de chevalerie). What&nbsp;
was considered dishonorable or a violation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.920
tournament regulations thus depended heavily&nbsp;
on the specific circumstances and, above all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.520
on what contemporaries considered acceptable.
Over the course of their history, tournaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.720
became increasingly regulated. The loosely&nbsp;
regulated war games of the 11th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:11.120
evolved into a complex, staged competitions&nbsp;
with fixed rules—and the more rules there were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.440
the more opportunities there were to bend them&nbsp;
or get around them. So, someone had to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:23.840
the regulations were followed. From the late 12th&nbsp;
century onward, heralds increasingly took on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:30.320
role. They organized the contests, compiled lists&nbsp;
of participants, and ensured compliance with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:37.200
rules. Yet even with rules in place and someone&nbsp;
to enforce them, the line between permissible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.440
force and unfair tactics remained contentious.&nbsp;
One example of how tournaments did not always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.800
proceed in a chivalrous and orderly manner is&nbsp;
the Little War of Chalon, a tournament in 1274&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.560
in which the Lord of Chalon faced off against&nbsp;
Edward I of England. From the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.080
Chalon fought with extreme ruthlessness. He&nbsp;
deployed more men-at-arms than had been agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.000
and attempted to pull Edward from his horse by&nbsp;
grabbing him by the neck once hand-to-hand combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.880
began. While this was not prohibited, it was by&nbsp;
no means considered fair. However, the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.640
king held on to his saddle, and the battle grew&nbsp;
increasingly heated. When Chalon finally deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.400
foot soldiers and crossbowmen, he clearly crossed&nbsp;
the bounds of what was considered acceptable at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:33.200
tournament. Edward also brought in his infantry,&nbsp;
and the event turned into a bloody battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.320
To prevent such escalations, from the end of the&nbsp;
century onward, increasing efforts were made to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:44.480
ensure that spectators did not carry weapons and&nbsp;
did not enter the tournament grounds. In 1390,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.600
the city of Strasbourg, for example, stationed&nbsp;
500 guards where the tournament took place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:56.720
to keep the spectators and combatants separate.
