00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.440
In the 13th and 14th centuries, tournaments&nbsp;
became even more regulated, and various forms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.080
of combat emerged. In the ‘Buhurt,’ or melee,&nbsp;
large groups of horsemen demonstrated their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:42.480
skill. They typically fought with blunted weapons,&nbsp;
and great emphasis was placed on entertainment. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:48.720
more competitive variant was the ‘Turnei,’ or&nbsp;
tourney, a regulated, competitive reenactment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.640
of a cavalry battle. In addition, starting&nbsp;
in the 13th century, the ‘joust‘—a one-on-one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.320
duel between mounted combatants— also became&nbsp;
increasingly important. Depending on the occasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:08.000
combatants fought “à outrance”—with sharp&nbsp;
weapons—or “à plaisance”—with blunted weapons.&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:13.600
In the 15th century, the focus of tournaments&nbsp;
shifted from training to spectacle. Beginning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.440
the 1420s, the jousting lanes were often separated&nbsp;
by a barrier to prevent collisions, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:25.600
now a matter of honor to spare one’s opponent as&nbsp;
much as possible. Contests no longer necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:31.840
continued until there was a winner, but rather&nbsp;
for a set period or a set number of thrusts or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.440
blows. The rules became increasingly detailed,&nbsp;
and the winner was now usually determined by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:43.680
a point system: points could be awarded for a&nbsp;
technically sound thrust, the force of a hit, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:50.960
the skill of a parry. Those who struck below the&nbsp;
belt, on the other hand, received penalty points.&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:58.160
Nevertheless, there was still considerable room&nbsp;
for interpretation. In 1467, Anthony, the Bastard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.480
of Burgundy, traveled to London in great pomp&nbsp;
to accept a challenge from Anthony Woodville,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.720
the Lord of Scales. The opponents first met to&nbsp;
establish the terms of the contest and agreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:16.960
among other things, not to use spiked armor on&nbsp;
their horses. But when the two collided violently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:23.440
on the day of the tournament and Anton’s stallion&nbsp;
went down, a metal spike was found embedded deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:29.840
in its flesh. Suspicion immediately arose that&nbsp;
Scales had violated the agreement. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:35.440
the accusation could not be conclusively proven:&nbsp;
Scales claimed it was a splinter that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.760
accidentally come loose from his equipment.
To deal with precisely such disputes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:47.840
heralds and judges oversaw the contests. They&nbsp;
inspected the equipment before the tournament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:54.240
maintained order during the event, and ruled&nbsp;
on contentious cases. Sometimes municipal or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.960
royal officials also supervised the tournaments.&nbsp;
Emperor Maximilian I, who was an avid tournament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:07.040
enthusiast, employed tournament and jousting&nbsp;
masters specifically for this purpose. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:13.760
15th century, noblewomen also played an important&nbsp;
role. During a heraldic inspection, they examined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.680
the participants’ lineage, coat of arms, and&nbsp;
reputation. Only men of high birth whose honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.840
was considered beyond reproach were admitted.&nbsp;
For example, anyone who had cheated, broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:33.440
an oath, or dishonored a woman was barred from&nbsp;
participation. At the 1481 Heidelberg tournament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:40.560
a total of 90 out of the 441 registered&nbsp;
participants were excluded on these grounds.&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.200
Violations could now also be severely&nbsp;
punished. Minor offenses were punished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:52.160
with fines, technical rule violations with point&nbsp;
deductions, and serious offenses—for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.080
fighting with prohibited weapons—by confiscating&nbsp;
all of the offender’s tournament equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:05.120
In cases of breaches of honor, the offending&nbsp;
participant was publicly humiliated. In 1434,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:10.720
at a tournament in Schaffhausen, a certain&nbsp;
Martin Zöllner was accused of defrauding an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:17.280
acquaintance out of his inheritance. As a result,&nbsp;
the other participants pulled him from his saddle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:23.840
beat him with rods, and, to humiliate him, placed&nbsp;
him on the tournament barrier in his saddle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:30.080
where he had to remain for the rest of the day.
Yet despite all the checks and penalties, cheating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:35.520
continued. For example, there are recorded cases&nbsp;
in which knights riveted their armor to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:42.160
saddles, carried sharp weapons in “à plaisance”&nbsp;
combat, used lances longer than permitted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.560
or even wore special gloves that mechanically&nbsp;
clamped around the lance. Some allegedly went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.680
so far as to send their squires out the night&nbsp;
before the contest to ambush their opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:59.840
pull a sack over his head, and beat him up.
A common form of cheating involved tampering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:06.000
with the lances. In jousting, a lance that&nbsp;
broke was considered a sign of a successful hit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.840
For it to splinter, the thrust had to be precise&nbsp;
and delivered with great force—enough that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:17.200
in a real battle, it would have seriously wounded&nbsp;
the opponent or thrown him from the saddle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:23.040
To ensure a fair contest, there were strict&nbsp;
regulations: All participants had to use lances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:30.320
of the same size and design, fitted with identical&nbsp;
tips. Sometimes, only authorized craftsmen were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:36.800
permitted to make them. Nevertheless, there were&nbsp;
cases—such as that of Prince Ludwig of Bavaria—in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.960
which things were not entirely above board.
Some tournaments were unfair from the start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.560
because performance alone did not determine&nbsp;
the outcome. Among other things, tournaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:54.400
offered an opportunity for young nobles to make a&nbsp;
name for themselves, and organizers certainly had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:01.360
incentives to favor certain families. One case in&nbsp;
which social status, rather than skill, appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:08.880
have determined the outcome is that of Lorenzo de’&nbsp;
Medici. After a tournament in Florence in 1469,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:16.320
Lorenzo himself acknowledged as much: “Although&nbsp;
I was not a vigorous warrior, the first prize was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:23.440
awarded to me: a helmet inlaid with silver, and&nbsp;
a figure of Mars on the crest.” The tournament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:30.240
was likely a political display. Lorenzo’s father&nbsp;
had recently died, and the event offered an ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:35.840
opportunity to publicly reinforce his position&nbsp;
as head of the family and, thus, as the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:41.920
important man in Florence. Critics therefore&nbsp;
claimed that the tournament had been rigged.&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:47.280
A special form of tournament was the so-called&nbsp;
“Pas d’Armes” , a carefully staged spectacle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:53.040
was usually part of a courtly festival. Typically,&nbsp;
a single knight or a group of knights would defend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:59.680
a symbolic pass against several challengers. These&nbsp;
battles were embedded in elaborate stage sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:05.200
ceremonies, and narratives inspired by the&nbsp;
literature of the time. Popular sources of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:10.480
inspiration included, for example, chivalric&nbsp;
romances and the Arthurian legends. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:16.240
were events so elaborate that only the most&nbsp;
powerful and wealthy princes could host them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:21.840
Participation was also reserved for wealthy&nbsp;
nobles, as it required specially designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:27.040
tournament armor—such as the so-called&nbsp;
“Stechzeug” for the joust. By now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:32.000
the loosely regulated war training of the&nbsp;
High Middle Ages had evolved into a courtly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:37.440
spectacle in which the noble elite displayed&nbsp;
their lineage, wealth, and social status.&nbsp;

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:43.760
The emphasis on entertainment had become so great&nbsp;
that dramatic effects—which in earlier centuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:49.360
would have been considered unfair deception—became&nbsp;
a highlight of the tournaments. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:54.800
around 1500, Maximilian I had a special&nbsp;
set of tournament armor made so that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.800 --> 00:15:00.400
when struck, individual parts would detach&nbsp;
and be flung away by a spring mechanism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:07.440
This did nothing to improve the armor’s protective&nbsp;
properties, but it made for a spectacular display.&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:13.760
Let’s now return to Prince Ludwig of Bavaria. The&nbsp;
fact that an allegedly blunt lance had pierced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:19.600
Ludwig’s neck guard was highly suspicious—and&nbsp;
indeed, when the weapon was pulled out, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:26.160
discovered that it had a sharp tip. Whether this&nbsp;
was an accident, whether Hohenlohe sought to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:33.840
an advantage, or whether he intentionally injured&nbsp;
Ludwig can no longer be determined. In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:39.040
Ludwig’s opponent fled the tournament grounds&nbsp;
after the accusations were made, and the injured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:45.120
prince died a few days later from his wound.
Ultimately, medieval tournaments were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:50.560
fair competitions in the modern sense. Cheating&nbsp;
occurred throughout the history of tournaments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:56.240
but early tournaments had hardly any fixed rules,&nbsp;
and the line between skillful tactics and unfair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:02.800
behavior depended on contemporary expectations&nbsp;
and conventions. Only as regulation increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:08.960
did it become clearer what was permitted and what&nbsp;
was not. One thing, however, remained the same:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:14.880
anyone who wanted to win had an incentive to&nbsp;
exploit whatever leeway the rules allowed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:21.280
Examples such as William Marshal and Philip of&nbsp;
Flanders show that it was often those who most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:26.720
skillfully used the rules and conventions&nbsp;
to their advantage who succeeded. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:31.840
end, therefore, victory did not always go to&nbsp;
the person who fought best, but sometimes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:38.240
the person who understood the game best and was&nbsp;
willing to push the limits—or even go beyond them.&nbsp;

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:43.760
Before you go, a quick reminder that our new&nbsp;
